    a: "When a dense cataract or other media opacity prevents formal visual acuity or fundus examination, projection of light — testing whether the patient can correctly identify the direction a light is shone from — estimates whether the retina and optic nerve behind the opacity are still functioning well enough to justify surgery. Accurate directional projection implies grossly intact posterior visual pathway function despite the opaque view. This makes it the standard, practical test for visual prognosis before surgery in such an eye.",
